    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1002">

                <pb facs="0893" n="884" sameAs="frus:frus1940v02/pg884"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.75 National Telephone Co./360: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Spain</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bucknell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-05-09T12:00:00+01:00">May 9,
                            1940—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received May 9—8:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>114. My telegram No. 113, May 8, 9 p.m. I am informed by members of the Telephone
                    Company that a representative of the Minister <pb facs="0894" n="885"
                        xml:id="pg885"/> of Finance is now proceeding to Paris to resume discussions
                    with Morgans in an effort to obtain a loan<anchor n="89" xml:id="d1002-f1"/>
                    from that bank for the Spanish Government. Under the circumstances the
                    Department may consider it advisable to notify the Embassy in Paris of the
                    inconclusive results so far obtained with regard to the final settlement of the
                    Telephone Company case.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bucknell</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1002-f1" n="89" xml:id="d1002-f1-content">
                <p>The Department in telegram No. 60, May 13, 7 p.m., informed the Embassy in Spain
                    as follows: “There is every reason to believe that Morgans will not consider
                    under present circumstances the transaction discussed.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
